# Do not delete the database when running tests

PHPUnit must never use the app MySQL database from `.env`. `RefreshDatabase` migrates and **drops tables**; that is only safe on an isolated test database.

When running tests, use `php artisan test` or `vendor/bin/phpunit`. Those pick up `phpunit.xml`, which already forces SQLite in-memory.

- Keep `phpunit.xml` with `DB_CONNECTION=sqlite` and `DB_DATABASE=:memory:`
- Do not point tests at MySQL, `DB_DATABASE` from `.env`, or any shared/dev/prod database
- Do not run `migrate:fresh`, `migrate:refresh`, `db:wipe`, `db:seed`, or `schema:dump` against the app database as a way to “run tests”
- When adding Feature tests, keep `RefreshDatabase` (or equivalent) only while PHPUnit still uses SQLite in-memory
- Do not remove the sqlite overrides from `phpunit.xml` to “match production” or reuse existing rows

```xml
<!-- ✅ GOOD: phpunit.xml -->
<env name="DB_CONNECTION" value="sqlite"/>
<env name="DB_DATABASE" value=":memory:"/>
```

```xml
<!-- ❌ BAD: tests hit the same DB as the running app -->
<env name="DB_CONNECTION" value="mysql"/>
```
